# global: the partition every context lands in unless it names another, and the
# one an unconfigured mux uses. Override it by dropping your own in
# $MUX_DIR/partitions/global.partition.
#
# THERE IS NO `scan` LINE HERE, AND THAT IS THE POINT. This file used to ship
# `scan ~/src 3`, which contradicted the rule stated two lines below it: a
# location nobody chose is still a location nobody chose, whether it comes from
# a built-in or from a shipped file. It cost a `[FAIL]` on every machine that
# does not happen to keep projects in ~/src, which is most machines and every
# CI runner, and a fresh install reporting FAIL for having declined to guess is
# the worst possible first impression.
#
# BEHAVIOUR ONLY, NEVER A LOCATION. A partition nobody has configured gets no
# roots and therefore no map, so an unconfigured discovery is VISIBLE (`mux
# scan` says so and exits 2, `mux check` warns) rather than quietly indexing
# somebody else's idea of where your work lives.
#
# TO TURN DISCOVERY ON:
#
#     mux scan --init          asks where your projects are, and writes it
#     mux scan --init DIR      the same, without asking
#
# or add the line by hand to your own copy of this file:
#
#     scan    <dir> [depth]
